@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sh:   <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix prov: <http://www.w3.org/ns/prov#> .
@prefix assembly: <http://example.org/orexis/assembly#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/core> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Orexis core" ;
    rdfs:comment """The society kernel: agents, the capabilities they compose, and the versioned world they share. Everything else is a MODULE layered on this — the microcontrollers it runs on, the protocols their parts speak, the parts themselves, the capabilities, and the domain. Code may reference the terms defined here; it may never reference an instance.

What is NOT here any more is everything physical. `packages/orexis-part-microcontroller` holds boards, peripherals, pins and wires; a concrete part is its own package beside it. The two subjects were sharing a file and growing at different rates: adding a sensor should not touch what a society IS.""" .

#################  Agents and capabilities (composition, not hierarchy)  #################

orexis:Agent a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "A self-interested principal with a stake. What an agent can DO is not its class — it is the set of capabilities composed onto it, so two agents of the same kind may run different modules." .

orexis:Capability a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "One thing an agent can do, defined by a module: a vocabulary (this ontology), rules (its SHACL shapes), and the code that reads them. Declaring a capability is what activates the module." .

#################  The choir: a hook is a term  #################
#
#  A QUESTION the agent asks of every module it composes, or an event it tells them — and
#  whichever module answers, answers. Named by IRI, never by a Python string: the kernel
#  declares the BDI-shaped ones here, a package declares its own in its ontology (sensing's
#  verdicts on a reading, reporting's record), and `Agent.ask`/`Agent.tell` refuse a term no
#  ontology declares. A module answers a hook by decorating a method with it
#  (`agent.module.hook`); the kernel's defaults on `Module` carry the kernel's terms, so an
#  override by name inherits the term (a-hook-is-a-term).

#  WHICH ROW a hook belongs to — reactive, progression, deliberative
#  (layered-by-timescale-and-interruptibility). A property of the QUESTION and therefore of
#  every answer to it: `handle` is always reactive, `take` always progression,
#  `desireUrgency` always deliberative. It is stated here rather than in a directory because a
#  row partitions METHODS of one module and crosses the kernel/package line in both
#  directions — and because a term can be checked, where a directory can only remind.

#  WHAT THE KERNEL OFFERS is not declared here, and that is deliberate. A service is a Python
#  object and its CLASS says what it is — `Beliefs`, `Keeper`, `Metrics` — so a package imports
#  the thing it wants and asks for it, with no parallel naming system to keep in step. Seven
#  `orexis:*Store` terms were declared here for one commit and removed the same day: a term is the
#  right key for an EXTENSION POINT, which is a declared thing in the graph, and the wrong one
#  for an object you can import (an-injected-service-is-reached-by-term).

orexis:Row a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Row" ;
    rdfs:comment "One of the three cognitive layers, as a term. Latency and interruptibility are the axis: reactive is atomic and must never block, progression spans time and suspends, deliberation searches." .

orexis:Reactive a orexis:Row ; rdfs:label "reactive" ;
    rdfs:comment "Milliseconds, atomic, no search. Classify and write — decode a payload, take a bid into a round, note a status. Must never block and must never reach the planner." .
orexis:Progression a orexis:Row ; rdfs:label "progression" ;
    rdfs:comment "Seconds to minutes, suspends rather than blocks, searches nothing: carrying out what is already committed. BDI's own layer — intentions with a lifecycle, which is what holds a commitment across the gap between two deliberation episodes." .
orexis:Deliberative a orexis:Row ; rdfs:label "deliberative" ;
    rdfs:comment "The search: bounded, ranked by simulation, and the only row a model may be asked in. Should be interruptible; today a pass runs to completion, which bounded depth is what makes affordable." .

orexis:row a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain assembly:Extension ; rdfs:range orexis:Row ;
    rdfs:label "row" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which cognitive layer answering this hook belongs to. Every hook declares one, and `tests/test_hooks.py` holds them to it." .

#  `orexis:Hook` WAS HERE and is `assembly:Extension` — the mechanism by which anything reaches
#  anything is not the mind's, and the kernel keeps only the points it OWNS
#  (the-assembly-is-not-the-mind). `orexis:Row` below stays: which cognitive row ANSWERING belongs
#  to is a fact about this project's layering, and an assembly-time point has none.


orexis:desires a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(now=…) -> list['Desire']" ; orexis:row orexis:Deliberative ; rdfs:label "desires" ; rdfs:comment "What do you contribute to what this agent pursues? Answers are ranked together, one want per node." .
orexis:desireUrgency a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(desire, query, state, value=…) -> float | None" ; orexis:row orexis:Deliberative ; rdfs:label "desire urgency" ; rdfs:comment "How urgent is this want, in this world? The sharpest answer wins; None is no opinion." .
orexis:answer a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(keyed_class, key, carried, since, baseline, tolerance) -> Graph | None" ; orexis:row orexis:Progression ; rdfs:label "answer" ; rdfs:comment "What would an observation that ANSWERS this predicted fact look like? Asked for each KEYED fact a step predicts (a class some package declared `orexis:keyedBy`): the class, its key values and what it carries, as the step's prediction states them. A sh:NodeShape on the subject, conforming when a reading of the property later than `since` lies within `tolerance` of the predicted movement from the baseline — sensing's, because what a reading is is sensing's (#516, #518). The keeper holds the step on it; None is no opinion, and the first opinion wins." .
orexis:witness a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(keyed_class, key) -> float | None" ; orexis:row orexis:Progression ; rdfs:label "witness" ; rdfs:comment "What value does the world show NOW for this keyed fact — the reading a step predicted, as it stands at the verdict? Asked once per verdict for the residual (observed against predicted) the reviewer reads; sensing's, because what a reading is is sensing's (#518). None is no opinion." .
orexis:size a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(query, graph, row) -> float | None" ; orexis:row orexis:Deliberative ; rdfs:label "size" ; rdfs:comment "How big would the act this row commits to be? Asked of the row's taker before it is simulated." .
orexis:reports a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "() -> dict" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "reports" ; rdfs:comment "Which fields go on this agent's health point? Merged by the sink; a later answer wins a collision, and it is said." .
orexis:series a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "() -> list[tuple[str, dict, dict]]" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "series" ; rdfs:comment "Which tagged rows go into this agent's own bucket? Concatenated by the sink." .
orexis:sweep a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "() -> int" ; orexis:row orexis:Progression ; rdfs:label "sweep" ;
    rdfs:comment "The clock has moved: retract whatever you hold that has stopped being true by it. Asked of every module on the agent's own housekeeping tick, because a fact that expires by the clock must not wait for an event some agent may stop receiving. Only the OWNER of a fact knows which treatment it deserves — retract it, keep and mark it, or keep it and let a want go cold (absence-is-not-retraction) — so the kernel asks and never sweeps." .

orexis:quiet a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "() -> list[str]" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "quiet" ; rdfs:comment "What did you expect to hear and have stopped hearing? Log lines, said once on entry and once on recovery." .
orexis:stepDone a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(act, intention, took) -> None" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "step done" ; rdfs:comment "Progression handed a committed act to its actors and this is the answer: took, or not now. Told on the executing thread, to whoever above cares — a lower layer says this upward as an event, never as an import (#452)." .
orexis:planFinished a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(intention, action, want) -> None" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "plan finished" ; rdfs:comment "The world answered as the act promised: the expectation on this intention was met. Told by the keeper; deliberation fills it to reconsider the want." .
orexis:planFailed a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(intention, action, want) -> None" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "plan failed" ; rdfs:comment "The deadline passed and the world did not answer as the act promised: the expectation on this intention is unmet. Told by the keeper; deliberation fills it to re-plan the want." .
orexis:beliefRevised a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(belief_term, value) -> None" ; orexis:row orexis:Progression ; rdfs:label "belief revised" ; rdfs:comment "One of this agent's beliefs has been re-picked — take it up, if it is one of yours." .
orexis:subscriptions a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "() -> list[str]" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "subscriptions" ; rdfs:comment "Which channels do you need? Asked by whoever holds the connection, once the session is up. The QUESTION is the kernel's; what a channel is carried over is the transport's." .
orexis:handle a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(topic, payload) -> bool" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "handle" ; rdfs:comment "Something arrived on this channel — take it if it is yours. Offered to every module; nobody taking it is logged." .
orexis:send a assembly:Extension ;
    assembly:signature "(channel, payload, retain=…, not_after=…) -> bool" ; orexis:row orexis:Reactive ; rdfs:label "send" ; rdfs:comment "Carry this to the society, and say whether it left. True where it reached a live session, False where it did not — a message with a DEADLINE is refused rather than queued when there is none, because a queue would deliver it after the moment it meant anything (publishing-is-a-goal-and-the-protocol-is-a-primitive). Told by Module.publish; answered by whichever module holds the connection." .

orexis:hasCapability a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range orexis:Capability ;
    rdfs:label "has capability" ;
    rdfs:comment "Composed onto the agent in the WORLD graph (public: peers must know what a counterparty can do). The PARAMETERS of a capability are private, in the agent's own beliefs." .

orexis:actsFor a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "acts for" ;
    rdfs:comment "The subject whose interest this agent advances. Generic; a domain narrows the range (in the water domain, a Plant)." .

#################  Devices — dumb mechanism at the edge  #################

#  THE SUBSTRATE AND ITS STAND-IN WERE HERE — `orexis:Device`, `orexis:simulatedBy`, `orexis:DeviceModel`
#  and the physics a stand-in computes — and are `packages/orexis-part-device/`. A BDI kernel has no
#  business saying what a thing is made of: the substrate axis is real (SOSA is explicit that
#  its classes may be virtual, so `sosa:Sensor` asserts no hardware) and it is not the mind's.
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-substrate-is-not-the-minds.md.

orexis:ComputeHost a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Compute host" ;
    rdfs:comment "A machine that runs agent processes. Named so the world can say where its society actually executes." .

orexis:lanHost a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:ComputeHost ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "LAN host" ;
    rdfs:comment """How this host is reached by things NOT on it. A co-located agent reaches the broker at mqtt:brokerHost, which is loopback and must be, because host-networked agents have to agree on one name. A board on the wifi cannot use that name, so it needs a way to the machine running the broker. Two routes to one broker, and only the second is a fact about the network rather than about the society.

**A NAME, and that is the whole of why this is not `lanHost` any more.** An address here was a DHCP lease, and the old comment admitted it: "a fact with an expiry date". It expired three times — .126, then .130, then .133 — and the third cost a day, because a board went on dialling .130 and simply fell silent. Nothing in a world can be true about an address a router is free to reassign.

A hostname does not expire. `raspberry.local` is answered by the avahi already running on that host, and ESP32's lwIP resolves `.local` because `CONFIG_LWIP_DNS_SUPPORT_MDNS_QUERIES` is set in the framework this project builds against — so nothing in the firmware changes: `mqtt.setServer` has always taken a name. A router's own domain (`raspberry.speedport.ip` here) works equally well over ordinary DNS and is the fallback if multicast is ever blocked; it is not the default because it couples a world to a brand of router.

An address is still a legal value — some networks have no name service — and stating one is choosing to own the consequence.""" .

orexis:runsOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:range orexis:ComputeHost ; rdfs:label "runs on" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which host executes this agent. Documents why the bus is where it is." .

#################  Naming — stated, never derived  #################

orexis:localId a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "local id" ;
    rdfs:comment "The short name a resource is known by outside RDF. Stated here so nothing has to infer it from the shape of a URI. The ONE instance identifier a process is given is an agent's localId; everything else it discovers." .

#################  The store every agent keeps, and what it may cost  #################
#  Not a capability, and the distinction is the one AGENTS.md rule 2 draws: a capability is an
#  ability whose HOW could differ. Reviewing can be done by rule or by asking a model; compacting
#  cannot be done two ways. Keeping your own house is not a choice, so it is kernel — and it has
#  a clock no capability owns, because an agent granted no room to review itself must still
#  compact. See orexis/upkeep.py and issue #45.

orexis:BeliefBase a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Belief base" ;
    rdfs:comment "The store one agent keeps everything it knows in — the world it was given, what it has sensed, and its own beliefs. A class rather than a graph, because it is the whole store and not one graph inside it: what the agent BELIEVES is the sum of them. Named so that facts true of every agent's store have somewhere to be stated that no capability owns." .

orexis:maxBytesPerTriple a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "max bytes per triple" ;
    rdfs:comment "Above this ratio a belief base is mostly write amplification rather than knowledge, and is compacted. Stated here rather than compiled in, for the same reason the cadence bounds are: it is a fact about what this society tolerates, not about how the code happens to be written. It hung off the review capability until that left the kernel — a threshold every agent is held to cannot be owned by a package an agent may not have." .

orexis:BeliefBase orexis:maxBytesPerTriple 800 .

orexis:resignAfterS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "resign after seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long an agent endures a dead bus before it resigns and lets its container bring it back fresh (#53). The fault this bounds is the one that cost two days: a session that ends and never returns, inside a container that looks perfectly healthy, on a bus whose broker never restarted. Ten minutes is far past any reconnect paho would ever achieve — a link that flaps recovers in seconds and never meets this — and far under the hours a quiet failure used to cost. Stated here rather than compiled in, like the compaction ratio above: it is what this society tolerates, not how the code happens to be written." .

orexis:Agent orexis:resignAfterS 600 .

#################  Identity keys — published, because a verifier cannot read secrets  #########
#
#  The public halves of each agent's keypairs (#144, #145), written by `orexis-keygen` into a
#  generated `keys.ttl` beside the world's ratified files. Public by nature and in the world
#  because the world is the one place every member already reads: the host verifying a
#  presentation cannot read the presenter's secrets — that is the isolation — so the roster is
#  how identities are attested. Base64 of the raw 32 bytes, no PEM ceremony.
#
#  TWO keys because the operations differ and conflating them is the classic mistake: Ed25519
#  SIGNS (a presentation under the winner's own hand, #144) and X25519 SEALS (a claim only
#  its winner can open, #145). Absence is the pre-key era and stays legal — a world onboarded
#  before keygen learned agents behaves exactly as it always did.

orexis:signingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "signing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "Ed25519 public key, base64 raw. What a presentation is verified against: the durable identity exercising an ephemeral grant, per knowledge/decisions/authn-authz-capabilities.md." .
orexis:sealingKey a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "sealing key" ;
    rdfs:comment "X25519 public key, base64 raw. What a claim is sealed to, so the bus carries an envelope only the winner can open — the ACL keeps agents out, the seal keeps the bus out." .

#################  Who did it, and in what capacity  #################
#  A ROLE, not an identity, and the distinction is the whole point. There is no `orexis:Sovereign`
#  agent and there must not be: an installation has several users — one authors a world, another
#  operates it with fewer powers — and which of them was sovereign is a fact about ONE ACTIVITY,
#  not a permanent property of a person. PROV-O models exactly this with `prov:qualifiedAssociation`,
#  and expects the domain to name its own roles; naming one here is using PROV-O, not replacing it.
#
#  The role set is open on purpose. `orexis:Operator` and whatever else will be needed are absent
#  because nothing distinguishes them yet, and a role no activity cites is speculation.

orexis:Sovereign a prov:Role ; rdfs:label "Sovereign" ;
    rdfs:comment "Held by whoever ratified a world: authored its files and stands behind them. A role a user has in one ratification, never a kind of user — the same person may hold it for one world and not another, and an installation's other users hold none. See knowledge/decisions/who-put-the-fact-there.md." .

#################  The world and its versions  #################

orexis:World a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ratified world produced by genesis. Found by TYPE, never by a well-known IRI." .
orexis:WorldVersion a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "World version" ;
    rdfs:comment "One ratified version of the world's topology; the chain is append-only." .
orexis:currentVersion a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:World ; rdfs:range orexis:WorldVersion ;
    rdfs:label "current version" .
orexis:versionNumber a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:WorldVersion ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "version number" .
orexis:underWorldVersion a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "under world version" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world-version in force when this fact was recorded (provenance)." .

#################  Graphs — typed, self-describing (not magic strings)  #################
#
#  A graph IRI is an INSTANCE. `orexis:WorldGraph` is the term code may name;
#  `…/graph/world` is a particular graph and code may not. The classes below are the terms;
#  the instances at the end of this section are the data — and the split is what lets a reader
#  ask for "public knowledge" without any Python knowing which graphs those happen to be.
#
#  They are declared HERE rather than in each world because they are the same in every world:
#  installation-wide, like the shape of the store itself. Only the per-agent ones vary, and
#  those are derived from the roster (rules.ru) rather than written out.

orexis:Graph a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Named graph" .

#################  Three axes over one graph  #################
#
#  A named graph is classified THREE ways, independently, and conflating them is why some
#  graphs never found a natural home: what its content ASSERTS, WHOSE it is, and HOW IT
#  ARRIVED. A species' range is a constraint AND public AND derived; an aim is a desire AND
#  private AND asserted; an obligation is a constraint AND private AND received. Nothing but
#  the first axis is about meaning, and the first axis is the one the vocabulary lacked.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md. The classes below say what a graph IS;
#  which graphs are instances of each is data, asked for and never listed — the arrangement
#  orexis:PublicGraph already had, generalised to six modalities and five arrivals.

#  --- modality: what the content asserts ---


orexis:BeliefGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Belief graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What IS: the world as ratified, what the rules and the closure make of it, and what instruments report. NOT the graph historically called `beliefs`, which holds picks and no beliefs at all — that misnaming is what naming the axis exposed." .




orexis:PickRecordGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Pick record graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/beliefs/" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's record of picking: what birth authored and review has since re-picked, one value per term, plus the belief-proper terms (conversions, the wallet) the #297 sort left beside them. A RECORD class, not a modality — the desire modality derives its wants and projects this record in as a premise-and-content, so what used to need a modality typing to be copied is now read by name, the one construction from an agent's own id the rules allow. The write boundary is orexis:beliefsOf, unchanged." .

orexis:ObligationsGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Obligations record graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/obligations/" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's record of debts: claims won against it, presented or not, discharged rows kept because a debt paid and a debt forgotten must not look alike. A RECORD class — the duty-wants the desire modality serves are this record projected, and the prefix moved here when orexis:ConstraintGraph retired (#312)." .

#  `orexis:Mode`, `orexis:Chosen` and `orexis:Honoured` WERE HERE and are retired (an-action-is-one-node):
#  whether a row is the agent's own or a duty is whether its `orexis:available` query bound
#  `?for_agent`, and a second term for a fact one column already states was a second owner.

orexis:PromisesGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Promises graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/promises/" ;
    rdfs:comment "ONE agent's promises (#523, a-level-is-a-vocabulary-and-a-bridge): when the keeper reaches a step of an action nobody takes, the step's predicted fact is translated through the bridge into the vocabulary beneath and written here as an `orexis:Desire` the agent holds — `orexis:promisedBy` the step — with the bridge's estimate, so the ordinary road plans it over the actions that ARE taken. The step waits on the same fact; when its verdict lands the promise is withdrawn. A record, projected into the desire modality like the debts." .

orexis:MenuGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Menu graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the agent COULD do: the affordance rows, what others may demand of it, and what taking any of it would MAKE TRUE.

A ROW is derived and never authored — it is a conclusion whose premises are stored, so an authored row could outlive its premise, and that is the one hazard this modality must never reintroduce. A RULE about a means is asserted, from the file of the package that owns the lever, and is in no danger of outliving anything because it is a schema rather than a conclusion. Both are the same modality and they differ in ARRIVAL, which is what `orexis:arrivedBy` is for: the two axes were separated precisely so a class would not have to encode how its facts got there.""" .

orexis:IntentionGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Intention graph" ;
    orexis:graphPrefix "http://example.org/orexis/graph/intentions/" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent is DOING: commitments adopted and not yet resolved. Small and live, because what is finished belongs to history." .

orexis:PossibleGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Possible graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What WOULD be, if the agent did. The seventh modality, and the one a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs reserved when it said possible worlds are computed and dropped: belief is *is*, the menu is *could do*, and this is *would be, if I did*.

Nothing here is believed, wanted, committed to or done, which is why it could not sit under any of the six. A reader who mistook it for belief would think an agent had observed a reading no instrument took.""" .

orexis:HistoryGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "History graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the agent DID: resolved commitments, discharged obligations, doses delivered, amendments woken into. A bounded ring with the series store behind it — nothing points INTO history, because whatever must outlive the window copies what it needs at commit time, which is how an expectation already carries its own baseline." .

#  --- how it arrived ---

orexis:Arrival a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Arrival" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a graph's content got there. Five values and no sixth: everything in this system is said, computed one of two ways, done, or heard." .

orexis:Asserted a orexis:Arrival ; rdfs:label "asserted" ;
    rdfs:comment "The sovereign said so. True by ratification, not by argument." .
orexis:Derived a orexis:Arrival ; rdfs:label "derived" ;
    rdfs:comment "A package's rule concluded it from given facts. Policy: another society could rule otherwise with the same vocabulary." .
orexis:Entailed a orexis:Arrival ; rdfs:label "entailed" ;
    rdfs:comment "The vocabulary's own logic implies it — subclass, subproperty, hasValue, property chains. Not policy: any reasoner over these axioms would agree." .
orexis:Recorded a orexis:Arrival ; rdfs:label "recorded" ;
    rdfs:comment "This agent's own act, at runtime. Its word about itself." .
orexis:Received a orexis:Arrival ; rdfs:label "received" ;
    rdfs:comment "Another party's word: an instrument's reading, a peer's claim. The only arrival whose trustworthiness is a question — an assertion is the sovereign's by definition and a derivation is checkable arithmetic, but this either carries a signature or does not, which is why the graph classification and the trust story are one story." .

orexis:arrivedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ; rdfs:range orexis:Arrival ;
    rdfs:label "arrived by" .

orexis:graphPrefix a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "graph prefix" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where graphs of this class live, for the ones that cannot be declared: a per-agent graph does not exist until its agent does, so the vocabulary states the PREFIX and the instances are found under it. What this exists for is knowing litter from property — a graph nothing declares and no prefix claims is a ghost from an amendment, and dropping it is how a volume stops holding facts in a spelling nothing speaks. Declared by whichever package mints the graphs, which is why the sweep needs no list: it asks." .

orexis:PublicGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Public graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Knowledge every agent in a world may read: the vocabulary, the topology, and what the rules and the RDFS closure made of them. This is the class `store.query` looks for — the default graph of every query is whatever is an instance of THIS, discovered rather than listed, so adding one is a vocabulary edit and touches no code. Membership is deliberately by declaration and not by exclusion: a graph nobody typed is invisible, which is the safe direction to fail — the other way round, a new private graph nobody remembered to exclude would leak into every query." .

#  The existing classes, now saying their modality too. Nothing moves: a graph that was a
#  WorldGraph is a WorldGraph AND a BeliefGraph, because "topology the sovereign ratified" and
#  "an assertion about what is" are answers to different questions.

orexis:WorldGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "World graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "Topology and composed capabilities: who is wired to what, and who can do what. Public, sovereign-authored. Covers what the sovereign ratified AND what the rules and the closure made of it — those are three graphs and one role, because what a graph holds and who put it there are different questions. The second is answered in PROV-O; see orexis/provenance.py." .
orexis:StateGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph , orexis:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "State graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The world's CURRENT STATE as this agent holds it — the graph an effect rule rewrites and a plan forks per step, and the one a met-test reads. The kernel knows only that: what is in it is the packages' word (sensing writes observations into it, RECEIVED, since a reading is an instrument's word rather than this agent's). It was `orexis:SensedGraph`, named for the one writer it had; the instance keeps its IRI so a deployed volume keeps its readings. What sensors read — a belief graph whose content RECEIVED, since a reading is an instrument's word rather than this agent's. Measurement only; never a judgment. NOT public: an agent's readings are its own." .
orexis:OntologyGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:BeliefGraph ; rdfs:label "Ontology graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "The T-Box: this module and every other, plus what RDFS entails of it." .
orexis:ActionGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:MenuGraph ; rdfs:label "Action graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What each MEANS makes true — one `sh:SPARQLRule` per lever, loaded from the packages at genesis exactly as the T-Box is, and replaced from those files on every boot.

PUBLIC and not belief. A rule here is a SCHEMA — what applying a lever WOULD do, stated once by the package that owns the lever — and schemas have always lived in the store: that is what the ontology graph is. It is not a belief, because nobody holds it and no agent may revise it; and it is not the menu, which is INSTANCES (this valve, this venue) and stays computed for the reason affordances are never stored — a stored row can outlive the plumbing it was concluded from, and a schema cannot.""" .

orexis:DeliberationGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:PossibleGraph ; rdfs:label "Deliberation graph" ;
    rdfs:comment """What the planner considered on its last pass, per goal: the levers it simulated, the urgency each world would have reached, which one it took and why the others lost.

THIS IS THE RECORD'S ONE EXCEPTION, TAKEN DELIBERATELY AND NOT AN EXEMPTION FROM THE RULE. Possible worlds are computed and dropped, because a stored conclusion can outlive the premise it came from — except where a reader outside the process needs one, and then it is materialised into a graph of its own class, cleared at the start of every pass, carrying PROV to what generated it, never public and never in belief. All four hold here. See a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs, "Possible worlds are computed, not kept".

The reader who needs it cannot get it any other way: pyoxigraph holds an exclusive lock on the belief base, so nothing outside the process can re-run the search to see what it saw. That is the same fact `orexis-ask` exists for.

NOT PUBLIC, and the reason is not tidiness. Which levers an agent weighed and rejected is a disclosure nobody decided to make, and a rival reading it would learn what its neighbour can nearly do.

And nothing may read it BACK: cycle detection lives in the search where it can be reasoned about, not in a graph the agent consults about its own past thinking. A trace that becomes memory is a conclusion feeding a conclusion, which is the failure the computed-not-stored rule exists to prevent.""" .

#################  What the planner considered  #################
#
#  The vocabulary of a deliberation trace. It describes a PASS — one goal, the levers weighed
#  for it, and the verdict on each — and it exists so a sovereign can ask "why did you not
#  water it" and get the working rather than the answer.
#
#  Why it needs terms of its own rather than reusing the intention ledger's: an intention is
#  something an agent COMMITTED to, and every word there says so. A candidate is a world the
#  agent looked at and declined. Spelling the two alike would make "considered" and "adopted"
#  indistinguishable to a query, which is exactly the distinction a trace is for.

orexis:Deliberation a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Deliberation" ;
    rdfs:comment "One pass of the planner, about one goal. Replaced whenever that goal is planned again — the graph holds the last pass and never two, because a trace that outlives its pass is a conclusion sitting beside beliefs it can contradict." .

orexis:Candidate a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Candidate" ;
    rdfs:comment "One lever, simulated. Carries the world it would have reached as a number and the reason it was or was not taken — the same lever may appear twice in one pass at different depths, because what a dose is worth depends on where it is taken from." .

orexis:deliberatedOn a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ;
    rdfs:label "deliberated on" ;
    rdfs:comment "The goal this pass was about — the shape itself, so a reader can join the trace to the want and see what was being pursued rather than a label for it." .

orexis:considered a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range orexis:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "considered" .

orexis:chose a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range orexis:Candidate ;
    rdfs:label "chose" ;
    rdfs:comment "The candidate whose world the plan commits to reaching. Absent when the pass took nothing, which is most of them and is an answer rather than a gap: `orexis:verdict` then says which of the silences it was." .

#  NO DOMAIN, and deliberately: a verdict is said of a PASS (why it ended) and of a CANDIDATE
#  (why it lost), and they are the same idea at two scales. Declaring a domain would make the
#  closure type every candidate a Deliberation, which is worse than saying nothing.
orexis:verdict a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "verdict" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it ended as it did. On a pass: satisfied, improved, no candidate, exhausted, not better, refused — six answers where a planner returning a move or None could give two. On a candidate: whether it met the goal, improved on standing still, was worse, or landed in a world already seen." .

orexis:wouldReach a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "would reach" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world this lever would have made. The comparison the whole search turns on: a candidate is worth taking only if this is lower than where the agent already stands." .

orexis:standsAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stands at" ;
    rdfs:comment "The urgency of the world the agent is actually in — what every candidate is measured against, kept so a reader need not recompute it to understand the verdicts." .

orexis:wouldTake a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Candidate ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "would take" ;
    rdfs:comment "The action this candidate would take. NOT `orexis:by`, whose domain is `orexis:Intention` — spelling a weighed lever with the word for a carried-out one would have the closure type every candidate a commitment, which is the exact distinction a trace exists to keep." .

orexis:through a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:label "through" ;
    rdfs:comment "The lever a step goes through. On a candidate, the lever it WOULD act through; on an act, the lever the plan's head chose — the same `via` column of the same affordance row, so the ledger says which valve or venue and not merely what kind of move. No domain, because it is stated of both and the closure would otherwise type one as the other. On a candidate: the lever the candidate would act through — the affordance row's own `via`, which is the PROV the record asks for: a trace says which thing in the world would have done this, not merely what kind of move it was." .

orexis:atDepth a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Candidate ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "at depth" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many steps into the plan this candidate sits. Kept because a lever rejected at depth 0 and the same lever rejected at depth 1 are different findings — the second says a chain was tried." .

orexis:tookSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "took seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long the pass took. The one figure a trace could not already answer, and the one most likely to change this design: a pass validates a shape per candidate, and `series()` re-plans EVERY goal on every reporting tick, so an agent's dominant cost may turn out to be the instrumentation asking it what it wants. Recorded from inside the pass rather than timed by a caller, because a caller would be timing the trace write as well and reporting the observer's cost as the observed's." .

orexis:asOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "as of" ;
    rdfs:comment "When the pass ran. A trace is about a moment — the readings it weighed were the ones held then — and a reader who cannot tell how old it is cannot tell whether it explains what the agent is doing now or what it was doing before the last reading arrived." .

orexis:blind a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Deliberation ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "blind" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether some lever was passed over for want of a stated effect, leaving the pass unable to claim it looked at everything. A verdict of `no candidate` beside this being true means the search saw part of the menu — which is why the deliberator defers to the reflex rather than reporting that nothing helps." .

orexis:ProvenanceGraph a owl:Class ; rdfs:subClassOf orexis:Graph ; rdfs:label "Provenance graph" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the other graphs ARE, in PROV-O. NOT public, and the reason is use versus mention: these are statements ABOUT graphs, so merging them into the default graph would make an ordinary `?device a ?class` start returning activities." .

orexis:beliefsOf a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Graph ; rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "beliefs of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which agent owns this graph — the write boundary, made explicit. The domain is orexis:Graph since orexis:BeliefsGraph retired (#299): the whole store is beliefs, so a class naming one graph inside it 'the beliefs graph' asserted nothing — what the graph at .../graph/beliefs/<id> actually holds is the PICK RECORD, typed orexis:PickRecordGraph for what it IS, and this property still says whose it is." .

##  The instances. Data, not terms — code names the classes above and finds these by asking.
##  Three world graphs and two ontology graphs, because the role is what a graph HOLDS and how
##  a fact got there is a separate question — which each instance now answers with orexis:arrivedBy
##  rather than by having its arrival encoded in its name.

<http://example.org/orexis/graph/ontology>          a orexis:OntologyGraph ; orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/ontology/entailed> a orexis:OntologyGraph ; orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Entailed .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world>             a orexis:WorldGraph ;    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world/derived>     a orexis:WorldGraph ;    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/world/entailed>    a orexis:WorldGraph ;    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Entailed .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/sensed>            a orexis:StateGraph ;    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Received .
##  `graph/instruments` — what the agent holds about the instruments it polls — is declared in
##  sensing's ontology: a package may own a belief graph, and this one is its.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/actions>            a orexis:ActionGraph ;  orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Asserted .
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/provenance>        a orexis:ProvenanceGraph .
##  One pass per goal and no more: written at the start of each, replacing what was there.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/deliberation>      a orexis:DeliberationGraph ; orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Derived .

##  Where an agent says what its OWN graphs are. A per-agent graph cannot be declared in a
##  T-Box — it does not exist until its agent does — so the agent classifies its own at boot,
##  and it has to land somewhere READABLE — a reader asks what a graph IS and must resolve
##  it without naming any graph instance, which is the rule that keeps a query from silently
##  reading part of the truth. (The example this comment carried, a modality-scoped
##  `?d a orexis:DesireGraph`, retired with the modality classes: the desire modality is a STORE
##  now, and inside a store the graphs say only who put the fact there — #312.)
##
##  Public and belief-modality, because a classification is a fact about what a graph IS —
##  and the static graphs above have always been classified in the ontology graph, which is
##  public too. Putting the per-agent ones in `provenance` (which is deliberately outside the
##  default union, to keep mentions of graphs from answering questions about devices) was the
##  inconsistency, and it showed up the first time a scoped query could not see them.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/classification>    a orexis:PublicGraph , orexis:BeliefGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Recorded .

##  Where a world states a root desire: a TriG block in the world's own files, replaced from
##  them on every boot exactly as the world graph is, projected into the desire modality by
##  its build. Declared here so the ghost walk keeps it and so a world file needs no typing
##  line — naming the graph is enough, and a world may name instances.
<http://example.org/orexis/graph/desire/asserted>   a orexis:PublicGraph ;
    orexis:arrivedBy orexis:Asserted .



#  `orexis:Bounds`, `orexis:boundedBy`, `orexis:toleratedMin` and `orexis:toleratedMax` were here and are GONE.
#  They said that what an agent is held to is a kind of THING with two pairs of numbers; it is a
#  kind of FORCE, and the numbers belong to shapes that already knew how to carry them. The
#  region became a shape at orexis:ShouldBecome and the envelope one at sh:Warning, so what used to
#  need four terms and a class now needs none. See a-desire-is-a-shape.

#################  What an agent pursues, and with what force  #################
#
#  A desire is a SHAPE (a-desire-is-a-shape): the one language this project already had for
#  saying what a graph should look like, so constraint, desire and obligation stop being three
#  structures and become one, differing in SEVERITY and in whose graph they sit.
#
#  `sh:Violation` is the force the vocabulary already had — illegitimate, refused. This adds
#  the one it lacked: a shape whose violation is neither illegal nor merely noteworthy but a
#  GAP, something an agent is trying to close. `conforms()` has let non-violations through
#  since it was written, so an agent whose pot is dry starts, wants, and acts — which is the
#  whole point of a desire being a shape rather than a rule.

#  `orexis:ShouldBecome` WAS HERE and is retired (#472). It marked a shape as a want in the era
#  when a desire WAS a bare shape and needed telling apart from law; a want is a NODE now,
#  known by carrying `orexis:metWhen`, so the mark was redundant — and severity keeps exactly
#  two values, both claims about NOW: `sh:Violation` refuses, `sh:Warning` is the operator's.
#  A want's met-test enters no validation pass (the metWhen linkage takes it out, in
#  `conformance.py`), so it needs no severity to be survivable; its time-meaning moved to
#  `orexis:bindsWhen`.

#  WHICH WAY a want is unmet, said by the shape rather than worked out by whoever reads the
#  result. A validation result carries `sh:sourceConstraintComponent`, and after the split both
#  sides fail the SAME component (`QualifiedMaxCount`) — so the component cannot tell them apart
#  and something on the shape must. This is that something, and it is what a means will match
#  against when it declares which violations it repairs (#239): watering repairs Below, a fan
#  repairs Above, and `market:direction` stops being a hardcoded bit in a reflex.

orexis:Side a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Side" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which way a want is unmet. Three values and not two, because not having looked is its own answer rather than a point on the number line." .

orexis:Below a orexis:Side ; rdfs:label "below" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading sits under the floor of the region, or under the survival envelope. The side a lever that RAISES the property repairs." .
orexis:Above a orexis:Side ; rdfs:label "above" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading sits over the ceiling. The side a lever that LOWERS the property repairs — and for soil moisture in this domain, no lever does: a drowning plant is a real want with no answer, which is worth being able to say." .
orexis:Stale a orexis:Side ; rdfs:label "stale" ;
    rdfs:comment "A reading exists and stopped being evidence about now — older than the horizon this agent publishes for that sensor. Distinct from `orexis:Unmeasured`, which is having no reading at all, because the two differ in fact and an agent should be able to say which: one has never looked, the other looked and let the answer go cold. Both are repaired by the same move, and by no other — no lever moves a number you cannot see." .

orexis:Unmeasured a orexis:Side ; rdfs:label "unmeasured" ;
    rdfs:comment "Nothing has been read. Not a side of the range at all, which is why it is a value here rather than an absence: the repair is to LOOK, and the lever that answers it is Observe rather than anything that moves the world." .

orexis:violationIs a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain sh:PropertyShape ; rdfs:range orexis:Side ;
    rdfs:label "violation is" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it MEANS when this property shape is violated. On the shape, so the answer travels with the constraint that produced the result and cannot be recomputed differently by two readers. The alternative was for every consumer to compare the value against the bounds again — which is what the module, the query and the dashboard each did separately, and which is how a fern above its region came to be reported as one unmet want indistinguishable from a fern dying of thirst." .

#################  What a lever MAKES TRUE  #################
#
#  A means states its effect as a CONSTRUCT: the triples that applying it would add. The
#  vocabulary is SHACL-AF's — `sh:construct` for the query as a literal — because a rule with a
#  construct IS the action schema this needed (`sh:condition` was carried too, and went: the
#  precondition is the action's `orexis:available` query, whole, and a second statement of it that
#  nothing evaluated was a statement that could disagree), already RDF, already persisted,
#  already readable by a model and a sovereign. Nothing was invented here except the one thing
#  the spec has no word for.
#
#  WHY A CONSTRUCT rather than add/delete patterns, which is the textbook form: opening a valve
#  ADDS NO TRIPLE. It changes a number a later observation reports, and no template of concrete
#  triples can say that without predicting the number. A CONSTRUCT can, because this project
#  already computes that prediction for verification.

orexis:retracts a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "retracts" ;
    rdfs:comment """A CONSTRUCT whose result is the triples this effect REMOVES. Ours, because SHACL-AF has none: its rules exist to add entailments, so the standard has no way to say a thing stops being true. A simulator computes `(beliefs - retracts) + adds`, which keeps the whole operation side-effect free — nothing is deleted anywhere, two graphs are subtracted.

It is not optional, and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste. `sensed_writer` does DELETE-then-INSERT on ONE deterministic observation node per (subject, property), so a reading REPLACES its predecessor rather than joining it. An effect that predicts a new reading without retracting the node it replaces leaves that node carrying two `sosa:hasSimpleResult` values — and the survival envelope is `sh:qualifiedMaxCount 0` over readings past its edge, so a stale bad reading makes a possible world report a catastrophe in a world the plan has just fixed, and the planner rejects the plan that works. It fails the other way too: a stale GOOD reading satisfies a region goal the predicted value misses.""" .

#################  WHEN an effect lands, and how you would know  #################
#
#  A planner must know two things a rule did not say: when it may RE-DECIDE — never before an
#  effect could have landed, which states the flooding failure as a rule instead of patching it
#  with patience — and when to call an act failed, which is when "nothing happened" stops being
#  impatience and becomes a verdict.
#
#  Both figures existed and neither was a property of the effect. Actuation deadlined a dose at
#  `cmd.seconds + doseGraceS`, sensing computed `stale_after_s` from the cadence it commanded,
#  the keeper used `patienceS` and its own docstring called that a seam — "the dose and the
#  physics could derive a better one". Each was right where it sat, and a planner could read
#  none of them.
#
#  This is the argument #238 made for magnitude, one axis over. `cmd.seconds + grace` is a claim
#  about when the world should have answered; if a planner computes its own version, the agent
#  plans against one timeline and verifies against another, and the disagreement surfaces as
#  false UNMET verdicts — the same failure arriving through the clock instead of the arithmetic.

orexis:landsAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "lands after" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SELECT yielding `?seconds`: how long after the act until the WORLD CHANGE is complete. A query and not a number, because the duration is a function of the act rather than a property of the lever — a valve open for a two-litre dose is open longer than for a half-litre one, and `min(litres, cap) / calibration` is exactly the arithmetic the effect rule already performs. Its parameters are the effect's own, so one text answers for any act it could be asked about.

ZERO is a real answer and the honest one for a look. Observing changes nothing about the world; what a sensing act delays is KNOWLEDGE, and that is the confirmation route's business rather than this one's. A rule that omitted this would be claiming immediacy by silence, so the shapes ask for it.

WHAT IT DOES NOT INCLUDE, said here because the name could be read as promising it: SETTLING. This is delivery — when the valve stops — and not when the world has reached a steady state a reading would report. Three terms separate an act from its evidence: delivery (here, from the device's own calibration), absorption (the pot's, and stated in NO world today), and the next reading (the cadence the agent commanded plus its grace). Only the first and third are facts anyone has written down, and the sensor's cadence has been covering for the second by coincidence — a plant in trouble reads every thirty seconds, which is longer than a small pot takes to absorb a dose. A planner that wants to know when its work would become visible is wrong by exactly the missing term, and the honest first step is that somebody must say what these pots do, not that the code should pick a number.""" .

orexis:costs a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "costs" ;
    rdfs:comment """A SELECT yielding `?cost`: what taking this act spends, in the wallet's unit — euros, the unit of account bids are already priced in. Declared by the package that owns the action, exactly as `orexis:landsAfter` declares its timing and for the same single-source reason: a search that invented a price would manufacture findings, and nothing upstream may pin a price on a specific act — the cost shapes the RANKING and nothing else (#466, control-the-derivative).

ABSENCE IS FREE, and that is a statement rather than a gap — the reading an omitted effect and an omitted timing already get. A look's energy and a dose's spent litres are real and unpriced today; each is one declaration in its owning package's file the day a world cares.

WHERE IT RANKS: under urgency, lexicographically. Cost speaks only between candidates urgency cannot separate — same urgency, cheaper wins — because wellbeing and money are different currencies and a scalar that mixed them could rank but never explain. A plan no better than standing still stays refused however cheap it is.""" .






#  `orexis:effectOf` WAS HERE. An effect is a part of an ACTION now, and the action names its means
#  with `orexis:means`; see the block beside orexis:Means.

orexis:holds a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "holds" ;
    rdfs:comment "What this agent is held to or holds to: an `orexis:Desire` (which carries its met-shape and its measure), or a bare shape like the survival envelope, whose violation is a warning rather than a want. Replaces `orexis:boundedBy`, which named only the first and named it as though bounds were a kind of thing rather than a kind of FORCE. No `rdfs:range` since the desire was reified: a held thing is a desire OR a shape, and a range naming one would quietly claim the other is not held." .

#################  THE MIND'S STATES  #################
#
#  What a mind CONTAINS — desires, obligations, intentions and the means they name — declared
#  in the kernel because they are the lingua franca: four capability packages already had to
#  name them (market's bidding and hosting, deliberation, actuation), and a term many packages
#  must name is nobody's package-private word. `orexis:IntentionGraph` holding `intention:Intention`
#  was a split brain the graph axes exposed.
#
#  WHAT STAYS IN THE PACKAGES is the HOW: `desire:Deducing` and `desire:Consulting` are two ways
#  of arriving at a region, `intention:Keeping` one way of keeping a ledger, and each capability
#  keeps its own parameters (a patience, a met-fraction). That is rule 2 unchanged — a family is
#  where the how could differ — and it is why universality was never the test:
#  telemetry-is-a-mandatory-capability settled that a capability every agent holds is still a
#  capability, and the mirror holds here, that a STATE no agent is obliged to have is still the
#  kernel's word for it. `device:Device` is a package's now, and most agents hold none.
#
#  See knowledge/decisions/the-mind-is-six-graphs.md.

#################  What a desire IS  #################
#
#  A NODE with room on it, since the measure landed. For a while a desire had no class at all —
#  it WAS a `sh:NodeShape` an agent `orexis:holds`, carrying `orexis:ShouldBecome` — and that form could
#  say when a want is met and nothing else. What it could not say is HOW BADLY it is unmet:
#  conformance is boolean while a want has distance, and a-plan-is-a-path-of-graph-diffs already
#  named the consequence — "a goal-shape must state its measure or the planner ranks repairs
#  blind". So the desire is reified: the shape's content is unchanged and hangs off the desire
#  node instead of being it, and the measure hangs beside it.

orexis:Desire a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Desire" ;
    rdfs:comment "One thing an agent pursues: a node carrying the met-test (orexis:metWhen, a SHACL shape, unchanged in content from when the desire WAS that shape) and a label a dashboard or the ask channel can print. Derived by the desire modality's build, never authored — the same discipline the shapes always had. What the node does NOT carry is a measure: how badly such a want is unmet is a capability's answer, asked through the choir (Module.desire_urgency) of whichever world is being judged, and the kernel holds no measure vocabulary at all — the comment after orexis:metWhen says why." .

orexis:metWhen a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "met when" ;
    rdfs:comment "The met-test: a SHACL node shape whose conformance says nothing is wanted here right now. Boolean by nature — it governs the outcome LABEL and a duty's discharge, never how hard to try. Validated exactly as it was when the desire WAS this shape: unfocused, over the shapes the agent holds. Its negative twin is `orexis:unmetWhen`, and a want carries one of the two, never both (orexis:MetTestShape)." .

orexis:unmetWhen a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "unmet when" ;
    rdfs:comment """The met-test's negative twin (#468): a want met by ABSENCE points here at the AVOIDED state — a node carrying one `sh:select`, the pattern whose rows mean the want is unmet. Ruled by the sovereign when the first build routed avoidance through `orexis:metWhen`: a met-test pointing at the bad state evaluates right and READS inverted — 'met when dry-and-hot' — and a term that reads wrong to every skimmer is the flag-that-eventually-disagrees, in the reader's head.

The contract of the pattern: `$this` is the agent and `$state` names the graph the readings live in, both substituted exactly as a measure's parameters are — in the PATTERN, never the projection, where a substituted IRI is a parse error — and everything else is an unqualified pattern over public knowledge. Run by the STORE'S OWN ENGINE against whichever world is judged, live or a candidate node, so entered-or-held is one text on one engine; a pattern that fails to run reads as unmet, the loud direction. Its urgency is binary — met 0, unmet 1 — which the four-measures table calls sufficiency where no intermediate worlds exist, and the kernel judges it with no capability in the room: a want that is pure ratified data has no behaviour to grant, and where nothing could differ there is a function, not a capability.""" .

orexis:about a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "about" ;
    rdfs:comment "What a want is ABOUT — the one node an action query may join a lever to. Stated by whoever derived the want, and opaque to the kernel: sensing says a region want and a freshness want are about an observable property, and its own action queries walk from there; a want that is about nothing (a debt, a call) states none and is joined to its rows another way. The kernel carries it from the want to the row to the rule (`VALUES (?want ?about)` in orexis:available, `$about` in an effect) and never reads it. It replaced the ledger's and the row's `ssn:forProperty`, which was the kernel naming a sensing notion (the-stake-is-sensings-want)." .

#################  When a want binds: the scope axis  #################
#
#  The third axis of the #467 sitting — force × provenance × SCOPE — in PDDL's words and the
#  kernel's namespace, never a pddl3: import: every derivation writes one and the planner
#  reads it, which is the promotion test met. Three members ship because three have
#  customers; `sometime`, `at-most-once` and `sometime-before` stay unminted until a want
#  needs one. The class mirrors orexis:Side: a value-typing class, not a mood flag — the moods
#  stay computed off the carriers, never asserted.

orexis:Scope a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Scope" ;
    rdfs:comment "WHEN a want binds along a plan. The planner is the reader: Always is judged at every state of a candidate plan — not at-end every loop, every step of each loop — AtEnd at the end-world alone, Within against the simulated clock." .

orexis:bindsWhen a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range orexis:Scope ;
    rdfs:label "binds when" ;
    rdfs:comment "The want's time semantics, stated by whoever derives it — no derived want defaults one (orexis:ScopedWantShape). On the DESIRE and never on its met-shape, and named as `orexis:metWhen`'s sibling by the sentence that defines the axis: the met-test says WHAT would satisfy, this says WHEN along a path that question is asked. NOT `orexis:scope`, ruled by the sovereign on being asked: an axis is never a predicate's name here — force has no `orexis:force`, being carried by severity and the met/measure split — and scope's everyday reading is what a thing covers, not when it binds. Scope stays the axis's NOUN and the values' type." .

orexis:Always a orexis:Scope ; rdfs:label "always" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every state of the plan is asked, not the end alone — and every tick asks reality again: the loop is the half of always the planner cannot give. The region and the freshness wants are Always." .
orexis:AtEnd a orexis:Scope ; rdfs:label "at end" ;
    rdfs:comment "The plan's end-world is asked and the road there is not. No shipped derivation writes it yet; it exists so the next achievement-shaped want does not land on Always by default." .
orexis:Within a orexis:Scope ; rdfs:label "within" ;
    rdfs:comment "Met before a deadline, or not met at all: a candidate plan's landing time is held to the room left (`orexis:expiresAt`, read with `orexis:owedAt`). The obligation's scope — a debt's room is time where a stake's is the envelope — and what closes the deadline-is-only-heat seam: a plan that would discharge after expiry is not a plan for this want." .

#  NO measure vocabulary here, and the absence is the ruling rather than an omission. Two
#  drafts put it in the kernel — first the measure's SPARQL compiled into the derivation, then
#  a kernel-owned measures graph and evaluator with the content in a package — and the
#  sovereign refused both: the core is BDI, and how badness is measured is planning-domain
#  machinery that does not belong to it. What the kernel does instead is ASK, through the
#  choir hook every module already answers (`Module.desire_urgency`): how urgent is this
#  desire, in this world? A capability that owns the question answers from its own
#  declaration — sensing's `measures.ttl`, in sensing's namespace, read by sensing's code —
#  and a want nobody answers for scores 1.0, logged. Promotion of a measure word into orexis: has
#  the repo's usual trigger: a word SEVERAL packages must speak, which today none is.
#  See a-desire-states-its-own-measure.
#
#  The INPUT is stated in schema.org's terms and the reuse is load-bearing rather than thrifty:
#  an operating range's condition says `schema:minValue` / `schema:maxValue` about a property,
#  and the deduction is MAX and MIN over those comparable numbers. What comes OUT is SHACL's
#  `sh:minInclusive` / `sh:maxInclusive` — so there IS a translation now, one, in `rules.ru`,
#  and it buys a want that the validator can check and a query can read without a second
#  vocabulary for the same claim.
#
#  `ssn:forProperty` survived both spellings. It is what a condition uses to say which property
#  it is a range of, and `sosa:ObservableProperty rdfs:subClassOf ssn:Property` makes it
#  well-typed on a shape too — which is why a reader can still join a plant's range to an
#  agent's want on one predicate.



#  THE AIM WAS HERE — `orexis:Aim`, `orexis:aims`, the pick inside a region — and is `sensing:Aim`,
#  `sensing:aims` now: a point in a property, checked against a range a subject states, read
#  by the measure that scores a reading. Every word of that is sensing's
#  (the-stake-is-sensings-want). `agent/vocabulary.py` carries the rename for a volume that
#  holds the old spelling.

#################  An obligation: a desire someone else sourced  #################
#
#  BOID's O, and the sovereign's reframe of #218: a claim presented against my hardware does
#  not bypass my wanting — it RAISES a want, with provenance saying whose it is. One kind of
#  motivation, one decision path, and an agent's whole conduct is desires pursued through
#  affordances. See knowledge/decisions/an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced.md.
#
#  The guardrail is the one every desire answers to: an agent may RECEIVE a desire and never
#  AUTHOR one. A deduced desire is derived from ranges the world states; an obligation is
#  derived from a signed claim the society issued. Both externally anchored, so the
#  self-satisfaction loophole stays shut from both directions.
#
#  WHOM I may owe is not stored at all — it is topology, derived like the broker ACL from the
#  wiring that says who may present to me (the market package's honoured.rq walks it). What is
#  stored is what I owe NOW, in a graph of my own, because a debt outlives the process that
#  incurred it: a host used to keep its issued claims in memory and forget them on restart.

#  There is NO Obligation class (#471). An obligation is an `orexis:Desire` whose premises are
#  a claim and a counterparty — whose it is is what prov says — and the subclass that used to
#  say so was a flag: the kind-is-read-never-flagged rule, applied one level up, to a class.
#  The properties below ARE the premises; readers match on them and never on a type, which is
#  also what keeps a volume written before the class retired serving its rows.

orexis:owedTo a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "owed to" ;
    rdfs:comment "The counterparty. Always an agent this world's wiring already connects to me — an obligation to a stranger is not a debt, it is a forgery, refused before it becomes a want." .

orexis:forClaim a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "for claim" ;
    rdfs:comment "The jti of the claim that sourced it — single-use there, single-use here, so a discharged obligation cannot be raised twice by a replayed presentation." .

orexis:presented a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "presented" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the holder has asked yet. An unpresented claim requires NOTHING of me — the holder is waiting for its own watch to be live — and a presented one requires acting now. It says whether a debt is PURSUABLE, and no longer how hot it is: heat is `orexis:expiresAt`'s job, so an unpresented claim near its deadline is a debt this agent can see coming and still may not act on. The two were one step while a claim had no deadline to be measured against." .

orexis:owedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "owed at" .
orexis:expiresAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "expires at" ;
    rdfs:comment """When the venue stops holding the claim this debt came from — the claim's own `exp`, kept as the debt's. Absent where the market stated no window, and such a debt never becomes urgent, which is right: nobody is waiting on it.

This is what gives an obligation its urgency, and the reason it had to exist as DATA before obligations could drive acts. A stake's urgency comes from a survival envelope — how much room is left before the subject ends — and a duty has no envelope, so the room it has is TIME: at issue the whole window is ahead and nothing has gone wrong, near expiry there is none and lateness is real. Read with `orexis:owedAt`, which is why both are kept: urgency is the fraction of the window that has run, and an agent holding only the deadline would have to guess when the window opened.

The alternatives were measured against this and both are recorded as the risk in an-obligation-is-a-desire-someone-else-sourced: a duty pinned at maximum is the honoured mode returning by another name, since it outranks a plant that is dying, and a duty with no heat at all is an agent that quietly defects while its ledger looks tidy.""" .

orexis:amountL a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "amount owed, litres" ;
    rdfs:comment """How much the claim is for, where the good is measured. Written by the ledger when a claim is recorded and read by the market's Apply and Offer effect rules, which will not discharge an obligation whose amount they cannot see.

DECLARED LATE. This term was in use — one writer, three SHACL rules reading it — and no ontology declared it, for months. It worked because both ends spell it the same way by hand, which is precisely the accident `every-term-in-its-own-house` warns about: an IRI nothing declares is not an error, it is a pattern that matches nothing, and here it matched because nobody had typed it wrong yet. It surfaced when the concatenation that built it was renamed and the guard could finally see the sixth form.""" .

orexis:dischargedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "discharged at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while it stands. The same two-timestamps-and-a-reason shape an intention and a revision already have, because a debt paid and a debt forgotten must never look alike." .


#################  What an intention IS  #################

orexis:Intention a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Intention" ;
    rdfs:comment """One commitment: one want pursued by one action, adopted at a moment, resolved at another or still standing. `ssn:forProperty` names the property — the same term a region, a condition and an aim already use, so the gap an intention is about is joinable to the desire it serves by the vocabulary everything else speaks.



The lifecycle is two timestamps and an outcome, not a state machine: standing is `adoptedAt` without `resolvedAt`, and how it ended is `outcome` — the same shape review:Revision has, for the same reason. A dropped intention keeps its `becauseOf`, because a commitment abandoned without a reason is indistinguishable from one forgotten.""" .

orexis:pursues a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ;
    rdfs:label "pursues" ;
    rdfs:comment """WHICH goal this commitment serves — the bounds an agent is held to, or the obligation a claim raised. A commitment keyed by (means, property) alone cannot tell two obligations about one property apart: a dealer owing water to fern and to tomato holds two Apply commitments that look identical, so satisfying one satisfies both and the patience absorbs the second impulse as if it were the first. That is the shape of the observation-keyed-by-subject bug (#one-agent-many-sensors), found before it went live rather than after — obligations do not yet drive acts, and this lands first so they never can while it is open.

Optional, because the first three means predate goals having names: an intention with no goal is keyed as it always was, and every old row in a ledger stays readable.""" .

orexis:by a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The ACT committed to — the filled action: which action (orexis:fills), through which lever (orexis:through), how much (orexis:quantity), for whom, and when. It named the action node itself from the-action-is-the-kind until an-act-is-a-filled-action gave the filled one a node of its own. Not a plan: the how stays with whichever capability takes it." .

#################  An UPSERTED belief, for the planner's signature  #################

orexis:keyedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain owl:Class ; rdfs:range <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> ;
    rdfs:label "keyed by" ;
    rdfs:comment "A predicate that IDENTIFIES a node of this class — the writer upserts one node per key. Declared by the package that writes such nodes (sensing: an observation is keyed by what it observes of what), and read by the planner's cycle detection, which canonicalises a keyed node to its class, its key values and what it carries — never its identity. The kernel used to spell sosa here by hand." .

orexis:carries a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain owl:Class ; rdfs:range <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> ;
    rdfs:label "carries" ;
    rdfs:comment "The predicate a keyed node STATES — the value that makes two worlds different. Everything else on the node (an instant, who made it) is not where a plan stands: a look that predicts the value it already holds nets to nothing, which is what keeps looking from being a new world every time." .

#################  What an action IS  #################

orexis:Action a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Action" ;
    #  EVERY ACTION IS AN EXTENSION POINT (#523): the method that carries one out is
    #  `@contributes(<the action>)` on the module that takes it, and the signature and the row
    #  are stated here once for every member. WHO takes an action is read off that
    #  contribution — the contributing class's capability, and its family — and nowhere else:
    #  `orexis:takenBy` restated it and retired as a duplicate, with `orexis:take`, the one
    #  hook every module implemented with an if-chain on the action inside. The gates hold a
    #  family to its actions — in the repo, at onboarding and at boot.
    assembly:signature "(act, desire, intention) -> bool" ; orexis:row orexis:Progression ;
    rdfs:comment """One way of acting, WHOLE — the STRIPS operator as one node (knowledge/domain/action.md), and the KIND of act itself, which an intention commits to and a row carries: `orexis:available` is its precondition, a SELECT whose rows are the affordances it puts on an agent's menu now; `sh:construct` and `orexis:retracts` are its effect, with `orexis:landsAfter` and `orexis:confirmedBy` beside them; who carries it out is the code's to say, by `@contributes`. Stated by the package that owns the acting, in its `actions.ttl`, and loaded into the action graph at genesis, so a planner, a sovereign or a model reads the whole tool list in one place and an author adds a way of acting by adding one node. It replaced three files (affordances.rq, honoured.rq, effects.ttl) and a fourth surface for what was always one thing.""" .


#  AN ACTION STATES BOTH TEXTS OR NEITHER (#506). One a plan may CHOOSE — the menu's kind —
#  states a precondition and an effect, and the gate refuses one that lacks either: the search
#  is the only road to a lever since the reflex was absorbed, and a lever the search cannot
#  simulate is one nothing can take. One an EVENT adopts states neither: no plan chooses it,
#  the event is its adoption and a trigger its taking, the afforder never asks it, and it
#  exists as a node so a commitment can name it (`orexis:by`) and a hold can carry an
#  urgency — the market's Presenting. The kind is read off the texts, not declared: the
#  sovereign weighed a class for the second kind and dropped it, since its whole content was
#  that "neither" be said on purpose, and a term nobody reads is annotation.
orexis:available a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "available" ;
    rdfs:comment """The precondition, as a SELECT over the world binding `?property ?via ?direction` and — where the row is a duty owed to someone rather than the agent's own option — `?for_agent`. `$me` and `$properties` (the properties the agent holds desires in) are substituted by the collector; prefixes are the store's. A row exists only where every hop of the walk holds, which is why the menu is the precondition language and chaining needs no `requires` of its own.""" .


#################  An act: an action filled in  #################
#
#  An action is a template. What is committed, taken and promised is a FILLED one — the lever
#  it goes through (`orexis:through`), the want it serves and what that is about (`orexis:about`),
#  how much, for whom, and when. Execution's word; planning's is the STEP, an act at its place
#  in a plan, which is never written anywhere. `agent/act.py` carries it, and the keeper writes
#  the node beside every intention (an-act-is-a-filled-action-and-a-step-is-its-place-in-a-plan).

orexis:Step a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Step" ;
    rdfs:comment "One orexis:Action filled in and PLANNED — not yet done (the sovereign's ruling, 2026-09-02: a plan is not executed, so its elements are not acts). The lever it goes through, the want it serves, how much, for whom where it is a duty's, its window, what the search predicted taking it would reach, what it waits for (orexis:until, orexis:untilNot), what counts as the world having answered it (orexis:answeredWhen), what follows (orexis:then). A plan is steps; an intention commits to them (orexis:step) and stands at one (orexis:by); a claim promises one. Minted per adoption, so it is also the place it fills." .
orexis:Act a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Act" ;
    rdfs:comment "The RECORD that a step was taken: which step (orexis:of), when (orexis:takenAt), whether anyone took it (orexis:taken) — history, and only history. Written by execution the moment the actors have been asked; one step may be attempted more than once, and each attempt is an act. What the world made of it — the verdict — is the intention's today and lands here as the ledger grows." .
orexis:of a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Act ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "of" .
orexis:takenAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Act ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "taken at" .
orexis:taken a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Act ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "taken" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether an actor took it; false is an attempt no actor could carry out now, the step standing for the next." .

orexis:fills a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ;
    rdfs:label "fills" ;
    rdfs:comment "Which action this act is an instance of — the template it fills. The one hop from a commitment to the precondition, the effect and the taker." .

orexis:forAgent a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range orexis:Agent ;
    rdfs:label "for agent" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whom the act serves, where it is a duty's — the counterparty a Serving act is owed to. Absent on an act of the agent's own." .

orexis:quantity a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "quantity" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much the act moves, in the good's unit — sized by whoever would take it (Module.size), never by the planner. Absent on an act that moves nothing: a look." .

orexis:notBefore a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "not before" ;
    rdfs:comment "The window's opening. Declared and unwritten: a held claim spent later — futures — is the first thing that would write it, and that is the market's decision." .

orexis:notAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "not after" ;
    rdfs:comment "The window's close — what every hand-kept timer was saying: a bid not after the round closes, a serve not after the claim's expiry, a look not after the round that wanted it closes." .





#  `orexis:Means` WAS HERE — a class of five individuals that nothing ever read. A means was the
#  JOINT between three surfaces (an affordance row, an effect rule, an intention), and
#  an-action-is-one-node made those surfaces one node; a name whose only job was to point at
#  that node one-to-one was a second owner of it (the-action-is-the-kind). The ACTION is the
#  kind now: a row carries it, an intention commits to it (`orexis:by`), a trace weighs it
#  (`orexis:wouldTake`), and the kernel's ontology keeps `orexis:Action` and no individual of anything.

#  EVERY MEANS WAS HERE and every one is its package's word now — `sensing:Observe`,
#  `actuation:Actuate`, `market:Acquire`, `market:Apply`, `market:Offer` — each declared beside
#  the action that carries it, as means.md always said a package could. `orexis:Means` went too
#  (the-action-is-the-kind): the kind is `orexis:Action`. What let each leave was the kernel's last reference
#  becoming a hook: sensing satisfies the look it takes; the planner sizes a step by asking
#  its taker; the bidder answers its own held-claim urgency; the deliberator's tests import a
#  package word from the package.

orexis:adoptedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "adopted at" .
orexis:resolvedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "resolved at" ;
    rdfs:comment "Absent while the intention stands. Its absence IS the standing state — no flag to keep in step." .
orexis:outcome a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "outcome" ;
    rdfs:comment "How it ended: satisfied or dropped. A string rather than a class per outcome, exactly as review:outcome is." .
#  A STEP HELD UNTIL A CONDITION (#512, #514, #516): progression's primitive. ON THE STEP — a
#  planned thing is what waits; the intention is the commitment (which want, why, when
#  adopted, when resolved); the act is the record of a taking, written when one happens. A
#  plan committing to a sequence (#510) links its steps (`orexis:step`, `orexis:then`) and
#  gives each its own condition. The keeper adopts a step with a SHAPE over the agent's
#  beliefs and a deadline; it re-asks the shape whenever a belief lands and keeps the deadline
#  on its scheduler; when the shape conforms the step is handed to whoever takes it, and when
#  the deadline passes first it is taken as lapsed or dropped, as the adopter said. The
#  market's held claim was this road, written inside the bidder with a private timer and a
#  per-reading check; a plan's next step is this road with the previous step's prediction as
#  the condition (#510). The condition is DATA, so a sovereign asking the ledger sees what a
#  step waits for.
orexis:step a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "step" ;
    rdfs:comment "Every step of the plan this intention commits to. `orexis:by` names the one it stands at now; `orexis:then` orders the rest." .
orexis:then a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "then" ;
    rdfs:comment "The step that follows this one in the plan committed to (#510). Written when the plan is handed down whole; the keeper moves `orexis:by` along it when a step's expectation is met, taking the next with no search, and stops when one is unmet — the tail is then abandoned with the intention." .
orexis:predictedUrgency a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "predicted urgency" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the search predicted the want's urgency would be in the world this step reaches — planning's figure, carried onto the committed step so the ledger says what each step was expected to buy." .

orexis:until a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "held until" ;
    rdfs:comment "The condition a step is held for, released when it HOLDS: a sh:NodeShape, released when its focus node conforms — compiled by the kernel into the select it runs (#514). Always a shape: a condition that is naturally a query is a shape carrying a sh:sparql constraint, the form SHACL already has, so the ledger holds one kind of node for one kind of thing. One of orexis:until / orexis:untilNot per hold, never both. Removed when the hold ends, so a taken act is an ordinary standing intention." .
orexis:answeredWhen a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "answered when" ;
    rdfs:comment "The step's COMPLETION condition (#516): a sh:NodeShape that conforms when the world has answered the act — an observation past the baseline, in sensing's words — as against `orexis:until`, its READINESS condition. Conformance is the verdict met; the deadline passing first is the verdict unmet, the false-knowledge datum. A plan handed down whole (#510) holds each step's readiness on the previous step's answer." .
orexis:untilNot a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ;
    rdfs:label "held until it stops holding" ;
    rdfs:comment "The negative twin (#514): a sh:NodeShape, released when its focus node VIOLATES — hold while the round is open, hold while the board sleeps. The mirror of orexis:until exactly as orexis:unmetWhen mirrors orexis:metWhen, and one form for the same reason." .
orexis:whenLapsed a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "when the deadline passes first" ;
    rdfs:comment "`take` — the act is taken anyway, as lapsed (a held claim redeems blind rather than never) — or `drop`, the intention resolved as dropped." .

orexis:becauseOf a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "because of" ;
    rdfs:comment "Why it was adopted, and — appended on resolution — why it ended. Prose for the operator reading a ledger, never parsed." .

#################  The expectation — the END, judged apart from the means  #################
#
#  `outcome` above says whether the MEANS succeeded: the claim arrived, the look came back.
#  It says nothing about the END — the commitment was to reduce a gap, and an agent whose water
#  never reaches the pot buys, records "satisfied", and buys again forever: transaction
#  confirmed, outcome never audited. Confidently dumb, structurally. Issue #131.
#
#  So an intention whose act should MOVE something carries an expectation: the value it left
#  from (the baseline, copied here at adoption because the sensed graph keeps only the current
#  witness — the ledger is what remembers), which way it should move (the same direction the
#  domain states on its valuation, #127), and by when. The verdict lands beside the outcome as
#  a SEPARATE fact: `endMet`. Satisfied-and-unmet is the false-knowledge signature — the graph
#  said this lever moves this property, and the world kept disagreeing.

#################  A method: the steps taking an action comes to  #################
#
#  HTN's shape (#523): an action the search chooses may be ABSTRACT — Acquiring is the decision
#  to enter a venue at a price — and the package that owns it declares the steps taking it
#  comes to, in order: tender, present. The search never expands them; it chooses the action
#  by its effect and its cost. The keeper expands a step of such an action at adoption into
#  its method's steps, the last inheriting the prediction, and steps through them on feedback
#  as it steps through any plan. Each step's action may declare what it WAITS for, as query
#  templates with the rule's own tokens ($me, $via, $about, $subject, $beliefs), bound when
#  the step becomes current: `orexis:readyWhen` holds it before it is taken, `orexis:doneWhen`
#  holds it after, and `orexis:lapsesAt` says when either wait is over. The protocol that was
#  a chain of reactions in the bidder's Python — an offer, a bid, a claim, a hold, a redeem —
#  is thereby a plan the ledger shows, and losing a round is a step lapsing, which drops the
#  tail and asks deliberation again.
orexis:method a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:label "method" ;
    rdfs:comment "The steps taking this action comes to, in order — an rdf:List of actions, each a step of the parent's lever and subject. Declared by the package that owns the action, and only where the steps are a PROTOCOL the search could not deduce: the market's tender and present have no effects a want reads. Physics is planned, not declared — a step of an action nobody takes at its own level is achieved by planning its prediction with the actions that are taken. A step of an action with a method is expanded by the keeper at adoption — recursively, a member with a method of its own expanding in turn — into one step per member, chained with `orexis:then`, each `orexis:partOf` the parent step, which stays in the ledger as the filling they came from; the last inherits the parent's `orexis:predicts`, since the end the search planned on is reached when the method is done. An action with no method is its own one step." .
orexis:partOf a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ; rdfs:label "part of" ;
    rdfs:comment "The step this one is a filling of: a member of a method expanded from it. The parent stays in the ledger, off the `orexis:then` chain, so a reader can recover the tree the flat walk came from." .
orexis:readyWhen a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "ready when" ;
    rdfs:comment "A SELECT template that binds when a step of this action may be taken — a claim held, a watch live. Bound with the step's tokens when the step becomes current and held as its `orexis:until`; a step whose condition already holds is taken at once. Lapsing takes the step anyway (`whenLapsed` take), as a held claim redeems blind rather than never." .
orexis:doneWhen a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "done when" ;
    rdfs:comment "A SELECT template that binds when a taken step of this action has done what it was for — a bid answered by a claim. Bound when the step is taken and held as its `orexis:answeredWhen`, so the keeper advances to the next step on it; lapsing is an unmet verdict, which drops the tail and asks deliberation again. An action predicting a reading needs none of this: its end is its `orexis:predicts`." .
orexis:lapsesAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Action ; rdfs:range xsd:string ; rdfs:label "lapses at" ;
    rdfs:comment "A SELECT template answering when a wait on a step of this action is over — `?at`, a dateTime such as the round's close, or `?seconds` from now, such as the sensor's horizon. Absent, or binding nothing, the wait is the patience." .

#################  A bridge: where one vocabulary's promise is the next one's want  #################
#
#  A level is a vocabulary (a-level-is-a-vocabulary-and-a-bridge). An action nobody takes at
#  its own level — hanoi's Move — is a promise the level beneath keeps: its predicted fact,
#  translated, is a want the actions that ARE taken can bring about. The BRIDGE is what
#  translates: declared by the package that joins two vocabularies (the tower's: a peg is at a
#  cell), never by the kernel, and applied to FACTS — a step's promise going down, its verdict
#  coming back — never to the world a search plans over, which is what keeps the outer search
#  at seven moves rather than thirty drives.
orexis:Bridge a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Bridge" ;
    rdfs:comment "Joins an abstract action to the vocabulary beneath it: `orexis:refines` the action; `sh:construct` translates the action's predicted facts, bound as $via and $about with the world at $state, into the lower vocabulary's facts; `orexis:estimates` names the lower package's estimate for the want those facts make. A taker-less action with no bridge is a promise nobody could keep (#532)." .
orexis:refines a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Bridge ; rdfs:range orexis:Action ; rdfs:label "refines" .
orexis:promisedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ; rdfs:range orexis:Step ; rdfs:label "promised by" ;
    rdfs:comment "The step whose promise this want is — a step of a taker-less action, waiting on the fact the want brings about. Withdrawn with the step's verdict." .

orexis:predicts a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "predicts" ;
    rdfs:comment "The change the search predicted this step makes: what its world holds that the one before did not, and what it lost — the same canonical facts the plan's signature is made of, as one JSON literal of two lists (`adds`, `retracts`). One declaration (#510): the effect the search planned on is the effect the world is held to, and the answering shape is generated from this rather than sized by the actor. A step the search did not make — adopted by an event, a look — predicts nothing, and nothing is expected of it." .
orexis:baselineValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "baseline value" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where the property stood when the expectation was adopted. Copied into the row, deliberately: the sensed graph upserts — one current observation per property — so the BEFORE of any before/after survives nowhere but here. The ledger is the memory of commitments, and this is the commitment's opening position." .
orexis:predictedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "predicted value" ;
    rdfs:comment "The one number the step's prediction carried — the reading it said the world would show — written beside the verdict so the residual survives the sensed graph's upsert. Only where the prediction carries exactly one number; a plain-fact step writes none." .
orexis:observedValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "observed value" ;
    rdfs:comment "What the world showed for that fact when the verdict was reached — asked of whoever owns the fact's kind through `orexis:witness` — met or unmet alike, since a shortfall at the deadline is as much evidence about the conversion as an answer. The reviewer publishes observed against predicted as residual evidence (review:Residual) and re-picks the conversion when they lean and the tolerance when they scatter (#518)." .
orexis:baselineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "baseline at" .
orexis:deadlineAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Intention ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "deadline at" ;
    rdfs:comment "By when the movement should have shown. The patience serves as the horizon until something derives a better one from the dose and the physics — a recorded seam of #131, not an oversight." .


orexis:endMet a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "end met" ;
    rdfs:comment "Whether the world answered as the step predicted: the shape held as `orexis:answeredWhen` — generated from `orexis:predicts`, an observation within the actor's tolerance of the predicted reading, a plain fact present and a retracted one gone — conformed before the deadline. False is the deadline passing first, the datum an affordance is suspected on." .
orexis:endVerifiedAt a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Step ; rdfs:range xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "end verified at" .


#################  What this society tolerates of a commitment  #################
#
#  Two figures that were `intention:IntentionCapability`'s, carried on the FAMILY because a
#  family was the only thing there to carry them. There is no family — every agent keeps a
#  ledger, so keeping is not one way of having an ability — and they hang on the class itself,
#  which is what they were always about: how many refusals make a claim suspect, and how long
#  a commitment may absorb a repeat impulse. (What counts as the world answering is the
#  actor's tolerance on its own prediction now — a package's pick, not a kernel figure.)


orexis:suspectAfter a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "suspect after" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many consecutive unmet expectations for one (means, property) pair make the affordance suspect. A fact about what this society tolerates before it stops trusting a claim, not about how the code is written." .

orexis:patienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "patience seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How long a standing intention blocks re-adoption of the same one. Within it, a second impulse to do the same thing is ABSORBED — that is the amortisation, and with a consulted deliberator it is the cost model. Past it, a new adoption supersedes the old, which is recorded as dropped with the reason. An OPINION, so it is each agent's own belief and review may move it; the bounds beside it are constitutional." .

#  HOW MUCH ONE PASS MAY IMAGINE, in worlds — the ceiling on what a deliberation spends, stated
#  in the unit the search actually spends: a fork in the imaginarium, whose cost per world the
#  runbook measures against the mutable slice. It was a DEPTH, a constant in the planner, and
#  under a breadth-first search depth was a ceiling on compute; best-first (#492) it bounds
#  only how far ahead a plan reaches, which nothing needs — a plan is re-derived every pass
#  and only its head is acted on. A pick like the patience: authored per world, never moved
#  by the agent unless a mandate says so, and the engine's own default where none is stated.
#  See decisions/a-pass-is-budgeted-in-worlds.md.
orexis:budgetWorlds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:Agent ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "budget, in worlds" ;
    rdfs:comment "How many possible worlds one deliberation may fork before it answers with the best it has found. The search is anytime — it holds the best world seen and stops here — so a spent budget answers with progress, never with nothing. NOT a depth: a world's cost is what its mutable slice makes it, measured, and a sovereign states this in that unit. An OPINION of the sovereign's about this agent, authored in its beliefs; the agent does not move it." .

orexis:minPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "minimum patience" .
orexis:maxPatienceS a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "maximum patience" .

orexis:Intention orexis:suspectAfter 3 ; orexis:minPatienceS 10 ; orexis:maxPatienceS 86400 .

#  HOW FAR THIS WANT STILL IS, in the unit its actions cost. The desire's term, beside the
#  action's `orexis:costs` — the sovereign's two-stage cut says urgency is the want's and cost
#  is the action's, and an ESTIMATE is the want speaking about cost, which is why it lives here
#  and not on any action.
#
#  It exists because a want that only knows MET from UNMET cannot be approached. Hanoi's is
#  binary: every world scores 1.0 until one scores 0.0, so nothing is nearer, `best` never
#  improves, and a search shallower than the whole solution returns NOT_BETTER rather than a
#  step in the right direction. A want that can say "three moves left" makes progress
#  measurable, and the search can be guided by it.
#
#  IT MUST NEVER OVERESTIMATE. The search is best-first on `cost + estimate` and ends the pass
#  when the head of its open list passes the cheapest achiever in hand (#492) — sound exactly
#  because the estimate is a floor under what any plan through a world would finally spend.
#  An estimate that overstates would let the pass end with a dearer plan than exists, and no
#  test would go red on the domain's account: the plan is still a plan, only not the best.
orexis:estimates a owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain orexis:Desire ;
    rdfs:label "what is left to pay" ;
    rdfs:comment "Points at a node carrying one `sh:select` that binds ?estimate — the remaining cost to meet this want, in the unit `orexis:costs` is stated in, evaluated against whichever world is being judged with $state naming that world's readings. Substituted and run exactly as an avoided pattern is: one declaration, the store's own engine, no Python anywhere. A want that declares none is simply not measurable this way, and the search treats every unmet world as equally far." .
